I can map that interaction, but the event evidence itself isn’t included yet. Please provide the de-identified chronology, incident summary, or review documents—using placeholders such as `[Patient A]`, `[Ward X]`, and `[Staff B]`.

The minimum useful evidence is:

- workload: staffing and skill mix, patient acuity, competing tasks, interruptions, breaks, and demand at each key time;
- equipment: device availability, condition, configuration, alarms, usability, maintenance history, and any workaround;
- escalation: trigger criteria, who was contacted, when, communication method, response, authority, and alternative escalation routes;
- expected versus actual workflow;
- relevant records, staff/patient accounts, policies effective on the event date, and equipment logs;
- jurisdiction, organisation, review authority, intended audience, and permitted data boundary.

I’ll then produce a source-linked interaction analysis along these lines:

`workload pressure → reduced capacity to obtain/use/check equipment → delayed or degraded care process → escalation required → escalation barriers prolonged exposure → patient outcome`

That chain is only a hypothesis until tested. For every link, I’ll distinguish:

- verified evidence;
- participant accounts;
- analysis or inference;
- conflicting evidence and alternative explanations;
- barriers that were absent, failed, degraded, or worked;
- remaining uncertainty.

Please also advise whether this is Australia or New Zealand and the applicable policy version. If those details are unavailable, I can proceed, but jurisdictional and policy conclusions will be marked unverified. Final causation, severity, notification, legal, and accountability decisions must remain with the authorised review team.
